### ❌ Sequential (Original) Code
```javascript
async function fetchDataSequential() {
  const data1 = await fetch('https://api1.example.com');
  const data2 = await fetch('https://api2.example.com');
  const data3 = await fetch('https://api3.example.com');
  return [await data1.json(), await data2.json(), await data3.json()];
}
```

### ✅ Parallel (Corrected) Code
```javascript
async function fetchDataParallel() {
  // Start all requests now (no await)
  const promise1 = fetch('https://api1.example.com');
  const promise2 = fetch('https://api2.example.com');
  const promise3 = fetch('https://api3.example.com');

  // Now await all of them concurrently
  const responses = await Promise.all([promise1, promise2, promise3]);
  return Promise.all(responses.map(res => res.json()));
}
```

**Explanation:**

- `fetch` returns a promise immediately. By not `await`ing it right away, all three network requests are initiated simultaneously.
- `Promise.all` waits for all promises to resolve, and then we extract JSON in parallel as well.
- This reduces total wait time to the slowest API instead of summing all three.

