- **`Promise.allSettled`** is indeed the better choice when you need resilience: it never short-circuits, so you can inspect each result (fulfilled or rejected) and decide what to do (e.g., log failures but still succeed with partial data).
- A **`try/catch`** around `Promise.all` (or `.catch()` on the returned promise) is essential in production to handle unexpected errors like network timeouts.

Here’s a quick example combining these ideas:

```javascript
async function fetchAllUrls(urls) {
  const promises = urls.map(url => fetch(url).then(res => res.json()));
  const results = await Promise.allSettled(promises);

  const data = [];
  for (const result of results) {
    if (result.status === 'fulfilled') {
      data.push(result.value);
    } else {
      console.error('Request failed:', result.reason);
      // Optionally retry or fallback
    }
  }
  return data;
}

// Usage with error handling
try {
  const data = await fetchAllUrls(urls);
  // process data
} catch (err) {
  // catches only synchronous errors or unexpected issues
  console.error('Fatal error:', err);
}
```

